INT. NIGHT-MARKET NOODLE STALL - TAIPEI - NIGHT

Steam, neon, rain on the awning. Plastic stools. A hot wok roars behind a counter.

MEI-LING CHANG (30s, vendor, hair under a net) tosses noodles. Her uncle UNCLE BAO (60s, apron, radio) works the till and hums. Two customers share a bench: a tall WOMAN IN A RAINCOAT (30s, tourist, damp map) alone on one stool, and JAMES LIN (30s, locally raised, camera bag) beside her.

Nobody speaks. Then:

WOMAN IN A RAINCOAT
Excuse me. Is this the line for the beef?

MEI-LING
There is no line. Sit.

WOMAN IN A RAINCOAT
I am sitting.

JAMES
(to Mei-Ling)
Two beef, please. And whatever she's having.

WOMAN IN A RAINCOAT
I didn't ask you to order for me.

JAMES
No. I just thought. Sorry.

UNCLE BAO
(over the radio)
Beef! Beef! Bring beef!

MEI-LING
(to Uncle Bao)
I heard you.

Mei-Ling slides two bowls across the counter. The Woman in a Raincoat takes one, smells it, closes her eyes. James takes the other and burns his tongue.

JAMES
(to himself)
Idiot.

WOMAN IN A RAINCOAT
You okay?

JAMES
It's hot.

WOMAN IN A RAINCOAT
It's soup.

UNCLE BAO
Is the rain stopping?

MEI-LING
No.

UNCLE BAO
Then I'll have another.

He laughs. The Woman in a Raincoat looks at James, holds out a hand.

WOMAN IN A RAINCOAT
I'm Anneliese.

ANNELIESE
(correcting herself)
Anneliese Vogt. From Hamburg.

JAMES
James. From here.

ANNELIESE
Everyone says that about here.

JAMES
It's true.

She laughs. Mei-Ling catches Uncle Bao's eye. He winks, turns the radio up, and some old love song fills the stall.

ANNELIESE
(to everyone)
This is the best noodle I have ever had.

MEI-LING
Ever?

ANNELIESE
Ever.

Mei-Ling doesn't smile. She slides a second plate of pickled greens across, free, and goes back to her wok.
